WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:08.170
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.160
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.989
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.789
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.230
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.300
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.140
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.980
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it’s part

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.320
three of our three-part mini-series on

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.099
the theme of “stories from North Korea”.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.110
In case you missed them, in part one,

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.650
we told the story of Otto Warmbier, the

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.450
American student who went to North Korea

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.880
as a tourist and never really came home.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.010
In part two, we talked about one of the

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.929
most brazen assassinations of the 21st

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.089
century: the murder of Kim Jong-un’s

00:00:53.099 --> 00:00:55.859
half-brother in Kuala Lumpur airport.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.889
And today, we are going to talk about

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.309
Kim Jong-il: the dictator, the film

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.250
fanatic, and the kidnapper of two of

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.330
South Korea's most famous entertainers.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.379
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:11.389 --> 00:01:13.920
minute and get right into it.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.065
It’s January 1978.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.090
A woman called Choi Eun-hee

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.289
is travelling to Hong Kong.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.970
She is one of the most famous

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.079
actresses in South Korea, a household

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.410
name, a star of dozens of films.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.360
She has been invited to Hong Kong

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.550
by a contact who has told her about

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.660
an exciting opportunity: a new film

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.250
project, and the possibility of

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.740
running a performing arts academy.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.060
It sounds promising.

00:01:49.839 --> 00:01:51.669
Her career has been going

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.259
through a difficult patch,

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.310
and she is keen to hear more.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:58.970
She is taken to a place

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:00.690
called Repulse Bay.

00:02:01.399 --> 00:02:03.390
It is a beautiful beach on the

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.969
southern coast of Hong Kong island.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.930
Shortly after she arrives, without

00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:13.410
warning, she is grabbed by a group of men.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.560
She is bundled into a speedboat.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.780
Before she can understand what is

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.450
happening, she is out on the open

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.770
water, speeding away from Hong Kong.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.139
Eight days later, she arrives

00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:31.560
at a port in North Korea.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.190
She has been kidnapped.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.250
And the person who ordered her

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.450
kidnapping is not a spy chief, not a

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.250
general, not a faceless bureaucrat.

00:02:43.719 --> 00:02:47.720
It is Kim Jong-il, the son of North

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.760
Korea's founder, and the man who will one

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.590
day become the country's supreme leader.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.260
He wanted an actress.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:57.569
So he took one.

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:01.810
Now, to understand why Kim Jong-il might

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:04.200
have done this, we need to understand

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:06.470
something about the man himself.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:11.330
Kim Jong-il was born in 1941, and

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.890
grew up in the shadow of his father,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.870
Kim Il-sung, the all-powerful

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.420
founder of the North Korean state.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.449
As you will know, propaganda has always

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:28.310
been vital for North Korea, and film has

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.440
long been one of its most important tools.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.270
The elder Kim, Kim Il-sung, was keen

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.239
that his son and heir receive an

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.280
appropriate education in how to make it.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.270
So, from his early childhood, he was

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.579
taken to film studios, introduced to

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:53.729
directors and actors, and exposed to

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.750
the machinery of propaganda production.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:01.120
It didn’t seem to put him off, and he

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.510
developed what can only be described

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.090
as an all-consuming passion for cinema.

00:04:07.929 --> 00:04:10.910
By the time he was an adult, he had

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:14.070
assembled a personal film collection of

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:18.409
around 20,000 titles: everything from

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.320
James Bond films to the complete works

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:24.789
of Elizabeth Taylor, Rambo, Friday the

00:04:24.789 --> 00:04:28.350
13th, and all the Academy Award winners.

00:04:29.389 --> 00:04:31.519
Of course, these weren’t available

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.539
to the North Korean public — foreign

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.080
films were banned — but the same

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.960
rules didn’t apply to the Kim family.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:41.630
And he would watch these

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.000
films obsessively, often

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.250
several in a single sitting.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:48.940
But he did not think of

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.490
himself merely as a consumer.

00:04:51.969 --> 00:04:54.590
He saw himself as a critic too.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.100
This even led him to write a

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.560
book about film theory called

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.709
On the Art of the Cinema.

00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:06.890
As you might expect of anything the

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:09.360
son of Kim Il-sung produced, it was

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.610
heaped with praise in North Korea,

00:05:11.986 --> 00:05:14.460
and came to be regarded as the

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.170
authoritative guide to filmmaking.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.590
Outside North Korea the reaction

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.700
was rather less enthusiastic.

00:05:23.309 --> 00:05:25.810
But perhaps surprisingly enough,

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.290
even some Western reviewers admitted

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.539
that it wasn’t all rubbish, and

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.720
there were some interesting points.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.549
Kim, it turned out, did know

00:05:37.559 --> 00:05:38.859
what he was talking about.

00:05:40.099 --> 00:05:42.469
He was also put directly in charge

00:05:42.719 --> 00:05:44.770
of North Korea's film industry,

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:47.590
functioning essentially as the country's

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.020
chief producer and screenwriter.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.110
Every film made in North Korea

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.230
passed through his hands.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:58.796
And he was deeply, painfully,

00:05:59.036 --> 00:06:01.136
dissatisfied with what his

00:06:01.136 --> 00:06:03.356
country's studios were producing.

00:06:04.476 --> 00:06:08.436
North Korean cinema, by the 1970s, was

00:06:08.436 --> 00:06:10.856
exactly what you might expect from a

00:06:10.896 --> 00:06:13.876
totalitarian state with essentially zero

00:06:13.876 --> 00:06:17.216
connection to the outside world: stiff,

00:06:17.466 --> 00:06:20.936
joyless, and relentlessly ideological.

00:06:21.956 --> 00:06:25.316
Every film was a vehicle for propaganda.

00:06:25.676 --> 00:06:28.546
Every story ended with the triumph of

00:06:28.546 --> 00:06:31.226
the collective over the individual, the

00:06:31.226 --> 00:06:33.736
wisdom of the party and the Kim family,

00:06:34.096 --> 00:06:36.456
and the heroism of the Korean people.

00:06:37.516 --> 00:06:40.036
From a cinematic perspective, it was

00:06:40.036 --> 00:06:42.256
not exactly Oscar-winning material.

00:06:43.196 --> 00:06:45.216
People also seemed to be

00:06:45.246 --> 00:06:47.636
constantly bursting out in tears.

00:06:48.506 --> 00:06:51.166
Kim couldn’t quite understand why,

00:06:51.596 --> 00:06:53.906
and would reportedly complain about

00:06:53.906 --> 00:06:55.656
this to his directors, telling

00:06:55.726 --> 00:06:57.756
them that “this isn’t a funeral”.

00:06:58.856 --> 00:07:02.626
Now, for all of his many faults, Kim

00:07:02.626 --> 00:07:05.856
Jong-il knew a good film when he saw one.

00:07:06.756 --> 00:07:08.986
And he knew the films coming

00:07:08.986 --> 00:07:11.086
out of North Korea were bad.

00:07:12.596 --> 00:07:14.896
He wanted North Korea to have a

00:07:14.906 --> 00:07:17.416
film industry that the world would

00:07:17.416 --> 00:07:20.196
respect, films that could be entered

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.726
into international festivals and win

00:07:22.746 --> 00:07:25.246
prizes, films that would make the

00:07:25.246 --> 00:07:27.966
world look at North Korea differently.

00:07:29.076 --> 00:07:31.126
He knew that what the country was

00:07:31.206 --> 00:07:34.686
currently producing didn’t stand a chance.

00:07:35.686 --> 00:07:38.226
The problem, he believed, was

00:07:38.226 --> 00:07:41.246
talent; the country simply didn’t

00:07:41.256 --> 00:07:43.676
have the directors, producers or

00:07:43.706 --> 00:07:46.136
actors to make anything decent.

00:07:47.506 --> 00:07:50.466
North Korean filmmakers had long since

00:07:50.516 --> 00:07:53.006
learned to produce exactly what the state

00:07:53.006 --> 00:07:56.836
expected: cautious, formulaic propaganda

00:07:56.836 --> 00:07:59.956
films, often with plenty of tears.

00:08:01.406 --> 00:08:03.826
To be fair, you can understand why.

00:08:04.336 --> 00:08:06.456
They had no experience of the world

00:08:06.466 --> 00:08:09.336
beyond North Korea, no understanding

00:08:09.336 --> 00:08:11.766
of what audiences elsewhere responded

00:08:11.766 --> 00:08:14.746
to, and no freedom to experiment.

00:08:15.456 --> 00:08:18.016
If creating something bold and new

00:08:18.456 --> 00:08:20.946
might mean you get sentenced to decades

00:08:20.956 --> 00:08:23.556
in a labour camp, well, it’s hardly

00:08:23.556 --> 00:08:26.176
surprising the machine kept on churning

00:08:26.176 --> 00:08:29.356
out the same old junk year after year.

00:08:30.586 --> 00:08:33.766
The solution Kim Jong-il arrived at was,

00:08:34.416 --> 00:08:36.796
in its logic, quite straightforward.

00:08:37.546 --> 00:08:40.076
If North Korean talent was not

00:08:40.136 --> 00:08:42.746
up to the task, well, he would

00:08:42.746 --> 00:08:44.396
get talent from elsewhere.

00:08:45.786 --> 00:08:47.476
The most obvious place to look

00:08:47.776 --> 00:08:49.336
was just across the border.

00:08:50.416 --> 00:08:53.016
South Korea had a booming film

00:08:53.016 --> 00:08:55.366
industry which had produced exactly

00:08:55.366 --> 00:08:57.176
the kind of internationally recognised

00:08:57.206 --> 00:08:59.756
directors and stars that Kim wanted.

00:09:00.836 --> 00:09:02.726
The director he had in mind

00:09:02.936 --> 00:09:05.676
was a man called Shin Sang-ok.

00:09:06.856 --> 00:09:09.366
Shin Sang-ok was, by any

00:09:09.366 --> 00:09:11.466
measure, a remarkable figure.

00:09:12.476 --> 00:09:14.316
He is often described as the

00:09:14.346 --> 00:09:16.176
Orson Welles of South Korea.

00:09:16.876 --> 00:09:19.266
He cut his teeth during the so-called

00:09:19.326 --> 00:09:21.786
“golden years” of South Korean cinema,

00:09:22.026 --> 00:09:25.576
in the 1950s and 1960s, and would

00:09:25.576 --> 00:09:28.946
go on to produce more than 70 films.

00:09:29.496 --> 00:09:30.996
He had founded his own

00:09:30.996 --> 00:09:33.246
studio, was internationally

00:09:33.256 --> 00:09:35.956
recognised, he was a huge name.

00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:39.996
He was also, by 1978,

00:09:40.396 --> 00:09:41.946
in a difficult position.

00:09:43.006 --> 00:09:46.156
His studio had been shut down by the

00:09:46.166 --> 00:09:48.436
South Korean government, which had grown

00:09:48.496 --> 00:09:50.556
uncomfortable with some of his work.

00:09:51.146 --> 00:09:53.226
His career had stalled.

00:09:54.436 --> 00:09:57.616
And on a personal level, things

00:09:57.876 --> 00:09:59.776
weren’t particularly rosy either.

00:10:01.056 --> 00:10:04.536
His wife of 22 years had divorced

00:10:04.536 --> 00:10:06.586
him, after finding out about a

00:10:06.596 --> 00:10:08.956
series of affairs and infidelities.

00:10:09.786 --> 00:10:12.376
Clearly, this must have been difficult,

00:10:12.696 --> 00:10:14.346
as it would have been for anyone.

00:10:15.026 --> 00:10:18.736
But this was made even more so by the

00:10:18.746 --> 00:10:21.826
fact that his now ex-wife, a woman

00:10:21.826 --> 00:10:24.406
called Choi Eun-hee, had been the

00:10:24.426 --> 00:10:27.236
lead actress in many of his films.

00:10:28.416 --> 00:10:31.946
This, of course, was the same Choi you

00:10:31.956 --> 00:10:34.106
heard about at the start of the episode.

00:10:35.536 --> 00:10:38.496
Kim Jong-il knew all of this.

00:10:39.046 --> 00:10:41.146
And he had a plan.

00:10:42.486 --> 00:10:45.036
The plan didn’t start with a telephone

00:10:45.036 --> 00:10:47.856
call to Shin and promises that a spell

00:10:47.856 --> 00:10:50.006
in North Korea would give his career

00:10:50.006 --> 00:10:51.746
the boost it so desperately needed;

00:10:52.546 --> 00:10:55.626
persuasion wasn’t exactly Kim’s style.

00:10:56.106 --> 00:10:59.736
The plan began with Choi, Shin’s ex-wife.

00:11:00.536 --> 00:11:03.156
She was the easier target, and

00:11:03.396 --> 00:11:06.516
her abduction would serve as bait.

00:11:07.996 --> 00:11:10.696
Once she had disappeared, Kim thought,

00:11:11.076 --> 00:11:13.046
Shin would come looking for her.

00:11:13.826 --> 00:11:16.806
And when he did, they would take him too.

00:11:18.086 --> 00:11:21.066
And that is precisely what happened.

00:11:22.176 --> 00:11:24.936
Shin Sang-ok heard that his ex-wife

00:11:25.106 --> 00:11:26.656
had disappeared in Hong Kong.

00:11:27.346 --> 00:11:28.876
He was worried, and he

00:11:28.876 --> 00:11:30.666
flew there to investigate.

00:11:31.816 --> 00:11:34.166
North Korean agents were waiting.

00:11:34.866 --> 00:11:37.076
He was seized, put on a boat,

00:11:37.496 --> 00:11:39.476
and taken to North Korea.

00:11:40.516 --> 00:11:41.876
He wasn’t told that

00:11:41.876 --> 00:11:43.046
Choi was already there.

00:11:43.716 --> 00:11:46.366
And in fact, she would have no idea

00:11:46.586 --> 00:11:49.136
that he was there for several years.

00:11:50.486 --> 00:11:52.606
What followed, for both of them,

00:11:52.866 --> 00:11:55.406
was an ordeal that combined elements

00:11:55.406 --> 00:11:57.916
of luxury captivity, political

00:11:57.926 --> 00:12:01.176
re-education, imprisonment and,

00:12:01.346 --> 00:12:03.886
eventually, something resembling a

00:12:03.896 --> 00:12:05.886
forced professional collaboration.

00:12:07.156 --> 00:12:09.506
Choi's experience in the early years

00:12:09.826 --> 00:12:11.806
was more comfortable than Shin's.

00:12:12.546 --> 00:12:15.486
She was housed in a well-kept villa,

00:12:16.006 --> 00:12:18.946
taken to films and operas and parties,

00:12:19.216 --> 00:12:21.546
given a private tutor to teach her

00:12:21.546 --> 00:12:23.906
about North Korean ideology, and

00:12:24.316 --> 00:12:26.936
treated, in certain respects, as

00:12:26.936 --> 00:12:29.176
a guest rather than a prisoner.

00:12:30.186 --> 00:12:32.126
Kim Jong-il asked her for

00:12:32.126 --> 00:12:33.696
her opinions on films.

00:12:34.076 --> 00:12:34.906
He took notes.

00:12:35.036 --> 00:12:37.306
He wanted to learn from her.

00:12:38.086 --> 00:12:41.346
He also, it appears, enjoyed her company.

00:12:41.906 --> 00:12:43.116
She must have been something

00:12:43.116 --> 00:12:44.426
of a breath of fresh air.

00:12:45.666 --> 00:12:48.796
Shin's experience was considerably worse.

00:12:49.656 --> 00:12:51.796
He tried to escape twice.

00:12:52.576 --> 00:12:54.446
On the second attempt, he was

00:12:54.446 --> 00:12:56.823
caught just twenty five kilometres

00:12:57.046 --> 00:12:58.296
from the Chinese border.

00:12:58.816 --> 00:13:00.426
He had almost made it,

00:13:00.906 --> 00:13:02.426
but it wasn’t to be.

00:13:03.696 --> 00:13:05.796
He was sent to a political prison,

00:13:06.246 --> 00:13:08.576
where he spent more than two years

00:13:08.786 --> 00:13:11.016
being subjected to interrogation,

00:13:11.336 --> 00:13:15.006
starvation, and ideological re-education.

00:13:15.936 --> 00:13:18.086
This involved, as he would later

00:13:18.086 --> 00:13:21.326
recall, hours upon hours of propaganda

00:13:21.326 --> 00:13:23.986
films, political lectures, and

00:13:23.996 --> 00:13:26.166
being forced to write confessions

00:13:26.436 --> 00:13:28.306
of his ideological errors.

00:13:29.516 --> 00:13:33.426
And then, in March 1983, Kim

00:13:33.426 --> 00:13:36.086
Jong-il threw a dinner party.

00:13:37.296 --> 00:13:39.676
Both Choi and Shin were

00:13:39.686 --> 00:13:41.366
brought to the same room.

00:13:42.526 --> 00:13:45.806
Neither knew the other was in North Korea,

00:13:46.526 --> 00:13:48.956
and of course, neither of them had had

00:13:49.006 --> 00:13:52.416
any access to the outside world, no way to

00:13:52.416 --> 00:13:55.146
know anything about the fate of the other.

00:13:56.436 --> 00:13:59.116
The reunion was deeply emotional.

00:13:59.706 --> 00:14:02.006
The pair stood looking at each

00:14:02.006 --> 00:14:04.816
other, unsure what would happen next.

00:14:05.926 --> 00:14:07.766
And then, moments later,

00:14:08.356 --> 00:14:10.466
Kim Jong-il walked in.

00:14:11.676 --> 00:14:14.313
He was, by the accounts of both Shin

00:14:14.313 --> 00:14:16.986
and Choi, not what they had expected.

00:14:17.606 --> 00:14:20.886
Yes, he looked like his official photos:

00:14:21.196 --> 00:14:23.806
short in stature, platform shoes,

00:14:24.116 --> 00:14:26.656
and with that fluffy, bouffant hair.

00:14:27.566 --> 00:14:30.366
But he was also energetic,

00:14:30.509 --> 00:14:33.996
full of strong opinions, and in

00:14:33.996 --> 00:14:36.466
his own way, kind of charming.

00:14:37.456 --> 00:14:39.206
He apologised for the

00:14:39.216 --> 00:14:40.906
circumstances of their arrival.

00:14:41.266 --> 00:14:42.386
He expressed his

00:14:42.416 --> 00:14:43.876
admiration for their work.

00:14:44.236 --> 00:14:46.416
He explained his vision for North

00:14:46.416 --> 00:14:49.246
Korean cinema and what he hoped

00:14:49.426 --> 00:14:50.916
they would help him achieve.

00:14:52.276 --> 00:14:54.796
He also, rather extraordinarily,

00:14:55.176 --> 00:14:57.603
suggested they remarry.

00:14:58.396 --> 00:15:00.076
He planned to officiate

00:15:00.106 --> 00:15:01.336
the ceremony himself.

00:15:02.086 --> 00:15:03.686
They had little choice but to

00:15:03.686 --> 00:15:05.616
agree, and the wedding took

00:15:05.616 --> 00:15:06.856
place the following month.

00:15:08.046 --> 00:15:10.106
And then the work began.

00:15:10.896 --> 00:15:14.896
From 1984 to 1985, Shin Sang-ok

00:15:15.096 --> 00:15:17.636
directed seven films in North Korea.

00:15:18.856 --> 00:15:21.639
Kim Jong-il gave him resources he had

00:15:21.639 --> 00:15:24.616
not had in years: a studio, a budget,

00:15:24.626 --> 00:15:27.726
a crew, and, crucially, a degree of

00:15:27.746 --> 00:15:30.596
creative freedom that had never previously

00:15:30.596 --> 00:15:32.666
been afforded to his filmmakers.

00:15:33.916 --> 00:15:36.996
Although he was, of course, the dictator

00:15:37.006 --> 00:15:40.446
of a harsh, authoritarian regime, Kim

00:15:40.516 --> 00:15:42.806
understood on some level that if he

00:15:42.806 --> 00:15:45.286
wanted North Korean films to be able

00:15:45.286 --> 00:15:48.066
to compete internationally, he needed

00:15:48.066 --> 00:15:50.196
to loosen the reins a little bit.

00:15:50.856 --> 00:15:54.606
If every scene, every frame, was dictated

00:15:54.606 --> 00:15:57.446
by strict propaganda requirements,  even

00:15:57.446 --> 00:15:59.996
the best filmmaker in the world wouldn’t

00:15:59.996 --> 00:16:02.046
be able to produce anything decent.

00:16:03.436 --> 00:16:06.106
And the results were, well,

00:16:06.446 --> 00:16:07.466
they were pretty good.

00:16:08.456 --> 00:16:11.386
A film called Salt, which was directed

00:16:11.396 --> 00:16:14.936
by Shin and starred Choi, it won Choi

00:16:14.936 --> 00:16:18.106
the best actress prize at the 1985

00:16:18.296 --> 00:16:20.336
Moscow International Film Festival.

00:16:21.056 --> 00:16:23.546
Another was entered into a festival

00:16:23.726 --> 00:16:25.436
in what was then Czechoslovakia.

00:16:26.686 --> 00:16:29.306
For a brief, strange period,

00:16:29.746 --> 00:16:32.146
North Korean cinema was producing

00:16:32.146 --> 00:16:34.506
work that was being noticed.

00:16:35.906 --> 00:16:38.616
But the most famous work from this period

00:16:38.866 --> 00:16:43.756
was the 1985 epic monster film, Pulgasari.

00:16:45.386 --> 00:16:47.926
It's a film modelled on the Japanese

00:16:47.956 --> 00:16:50.986
classic Godzilla, in which a giant

00:16:51.086 --> 00:16:53.346
iron-eating creature is summoned

00:16:53.526 --> 00:16:56.076
by a dying blacksmith and goes

00:16:56.096 --> 00:16:58.216
on to help a peasant rebellion.

00:16:59.156 --> 00:17:01.596
Shin brought in special effects

00:17:01.616 --> 00:17:04.446
technicians from the Japanese studio

00:17:04.656 --> 00:17:06.336
that made the original Godzilla

00:17:06.336 --> 00:17:09.806
films, and even hired the actor who

00:17:09.806 --> 00:17:12.536
wore the Godzilla costume in Japan.

00:17:13.636 --> 00:17:16.316
In terms of collaboration, it is a

00:17:16.326 --> 00:17:19.856
real historical peculiarity: a North

00:17:19.856 --> 00:17:22.796
Korean propaganda film made by a

00:17:22.816 --> 00:17:25.746
kidnapped South Korean director using

00:17:25.756 --> 00:17:28.186
a Japanese special effects team.

00:17:29.436 --> 00:17:33.106
It has become, in its way, a cult classic.

00:17:34.136 --> 00:17:36.656
It is also a monument to the sheer

00:17:36.906 --> 00:17:39.066
strangeness of what was happening

00:17:39.256 --> 00:17:40.826
in those years in Pyongyang.

00:17:42.006 --> 00:17:44.636
But, no matter the resources they were

00:17:44.646 --> 00:17:47.116
afforded and the international acclaim

00:17:47.476 --> 00:17:49.706
they may have been starting to receive,

00:17:50.306 --> 00:17:54.236
Shin and Choi had no intention of spending

00:17:54.236 --> 00:17:56.366
the rest of their lives in North Korea.

00:17:57.153 --> 00:17:58.923
Ever since that first dinner

00:17:58.923 --> 00:18:01.063
party, the pair had been

00:18:01.123 --> 00:18:03.063
quietly planning their escape.

00:18:04.263 --> 00:18:07.293
The opportunity came in 1986.

00:18:08.333 --> 00:18:11.413
Kim Jong-il sent Shin and Choi to

00:18:11.423 --> 00:18:14.443
Vienna, in Austria, to seek financial

00:18:14.443 --> 00:18:16.643
backing for a new international

00:18:16.683 --> 00:18:20.103
co-production: a film about Genghis Khan.

00:18:21.363 --> 00:18:23.613
The trip was supervised by

00:18:23.613 --> 00:18:25.613
North Korean minders, of course.

00:18:25.893 --> 00:18:27.713
But it was Vienna.

00:18:28.323 --> 00:18:29.573
It was the outside world.

00:18:30.083 --> 00:18:32.043
During a meeting with a journalist,

00:18:32.353 --> 00:18:35.193
the minders agreed to wait outside

00:18:35.443 --> 00:18:37.803
and give them some privacy.

00:18:38.583 --> 00:18:41.143
The couple seized the opportunity.

00:18:41.553 --> 00:18:44.603
They ran out and jumped into a taxi.

00:18:45.293 --> 00:18:47.293
But there was heavy traffic,

00:18:47.543 --> 00:18:49.413
and they could see their minders

00:18:49.633 --> 00:18:52.003
frantically rushing towards them.

00:18:53.273 --> 00:18:56.173
If they were caught, well, they would

00:18:56.173 --> 00:18:58.403
no doubt be on the first plane back

00:18:58.443 --> 00:19:00.943
to North Korea, sent straight to

00:19:00.943 --> 00:19:03.773
a prison camp, and would never be

00:19:03.773 --> 00:19:05.903
allowed out of the country again.

00:19:06.683 --> 00:19:08.123
The couple decided their

00:19:08.163 --> 00:19:10.123
only chance was on foot.

00:19:10.583 --> 00:19:13.703
They got out of the taxi and sprinted

00:19:13.933 --> 00:19:15.783
towards the American embassy.

00:19:17.103 --> 00:19:19.603
They made it, and immediately sought

00:19:19.663 --> 00:19:22.143
political asylum, saying that they

00:19:22.143 --> 00:19:24.003
had been kidnapped; they hadn’t

00:19:24.003 --> 00:19:26.393
gone to the north voluntarily, as

00:19:26.593 --> 00:19:28.483
they had previously publicly stated.

00:19:29.893 --> 00:19:33.063
Kim Jong-il, of course, was furious.

00:19:33.573 --> 00:19:36.013
He accused them of embezzling

00:19:36.013 --> 00:19:38.073
money intended for the Genghis

00:19:38.103 --> 00:19:40.633
Khan film, 3 million dollars.

00:19:41.113 --> 00:19:43.103
He banned all their North Korean

00:19:43.103 --> 00:19:45.413
films from being shown in the country.

00:19:46.363 --> 00:19:47.823
The work they had spent

00:19:47.833 --> 00:19:50.683
years producing was, from the

00:19:50.683 --> 00:19:53.323
regime's perspective, now toxic.

00:19:55.013 --> 00:19:57.803
As for Shin and Choi, they spent a

00:19:57.803 --> 00:19:59.543
couple of years in the United States

00:19:59.793 --> 00:20:02.153
living quietly under protection.

00:20:03.003 --> 00:20:06.529
But quietly didn't mean inactively.

00:20:07.523 --> 00:20:09.953
They had spent years closer to Kim

00:20:09.953 --> 00:20:13.133
Jong-il than almost any outsider ever

00:20:13.143 --> 00:20:16.243
had, sharing meals with him, discussing

00:20:16.253 --> 00:20:19.283
film, and watching him hold court.

00:20:20.203 --> 00:20:22.753
And Western intelligence agencies were

00:20:22.783 --> 00:20:25.823
extremely keen to understand what that

00:20:25.823 --> 00:20:28.643
experience had revealed about the man.

00:20:30.003 --> 00:20:33.153
And it turned out that Shin and Choi had

00:20:33.153 --> 00:20:36.553
been preparing for exactly this moment.

00:20:37.733 --> 00:20:39.653
Throughout their time in captivity,

00:20:40.083 --> 00:20:42.583
they had been carefully and secretly

00:20:42.793 --> 00:20:45.143
recording their conversations with Kim

00:20:45.143 --> 00:20:48.683
Jong-il, not primarily for intelligence

00:20:48.683 --> 00:20:52.653
purposes, but to protect themselves, To

00:20:52.653 --> 00:20:55.303
have proof, if they ever made it out,

00:20:55.663 --> 00:20:58.303
that they had not gone north voluntarily.

00:20:59.683 --> 00:21:02.293
Using a hidden device, they captured

00:21:02.373 --> 00:21:05.753
hours of him talking about film, about

00:21:06.063 --> 00:21:08.943
North Korea, about his own frustrations

00:21:09.123 --> 00:21:10.873
with the country's cultural output.

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:14.893
Unguarded, candid, speaking

00:21:14.903 --> 00:21:17.203
freely to people he trusted.

00:21:18.243 --> 00:21:20.503
One American official who heard

00:21:20.503 --> 00:21:23.333
the tapes said his jaw dropped.

00:21:24.293 --> 00:21:26.393
Western intelligence had never

00:21:26.393 --> 00:21:28.253
heard Kim Jong-il speak before,

00:21:28.613 --> 00:21:31.673
let alone privately and unguarded.

00:21:32.473 --> 00:21:36.133
It was, he said, an intelligence windfall.

00:21:37.493 --> 00:21:39.833
After a few years in the United States,

00:21:40.043 --> 00:21:42.283
the couple returned to South Korea.

00:21:43.103 --> 00:21:48.463
Shin died in 2006, while Choi, his ex-wife

00:21:48.743 --> 00:21:52.183
turned wife once more, died in 2018.

00:21:53.633 --> 00:21:55.893
They had been taken from their lives,

00:21:56.243 --> 00:21:58.753
separated for years, imprisoned,

00:21:59.133 --> 00:22:02.153
forced to remarry, and put to work

00:22:02.343 --> 00:22:04.573
for a dictator's vanity project.

00:22:05.653 --> 00:22:09.413
And yet, in a strange way, the years

00:22:09.433 --> 00:22:11.943
in Pyongyang produced some of the most

00:22:11.983 --> 00:22:14.203
interesting work of Shin's career.

00:22:14.993 --> 00:22:18.053
A widely acclaimed monster film made

00:22:18.103 --> 00:22:20.663
with a special effects team from Japan.

00:22:21.203 --> 00:22:23.433
A best actress prize in Moscow.

00:22:24.073 --> 00:22:26.203
Secret recordings of one of the

00:22:26.203 --> 00:22:28.156
most secretive men on earth.

00:22:29.503 --> 00:22:33.373
And as for Kim Jong-il, he got his films.

00:22:33.653 --> 00:22:35.283
He just didn't get to keep them.

00:22:37.453 --> 00:22:39.683
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:22:39.683 --> 00:22:42.813
episode on Kim Jong-il and the kidnapped

00:22:42.823 --> 00:22:45.373
South Korean filmmakers, and with

00:22:45.373 --> 00:22:47.743
that comes an end to this three-part

00:22:47.743 --> 00:22:50.533
mini-series on the theme of North Korea.

00:22:51.273 --> 00:22:53.193
South Korea is quite a popular

00:22:53.253 --> 00:22:55.133
country for this show; North

00:22:55.133 --> 00:22:57.133
Korea, unsurprisingly, isn’t.

00:22:57.373 --> 00:23:00.113
But if you are from the Korean peninsula,

00:23:00.443 --> 00:23:01.933
I’d love to know your thoughts.

00:23:02.163 --> 00:23:03.603
Do you remember the events

00:23:03.703 --> 00:23:04.763
in this mini-series?

00:23:05.123 --> 00:23:06.783
And what do you think might lie

00:23:06.783 --> 00:23:09.223
ahead for the future of North Korea?

00:23:09.593 --> 00:23:11.703
I’d love to know your thoughts, and the

00:23:11.703 --> 00:23:13.653
place for that is our community forum,

00:23:13.883 --> 00:23:16.653
which is at community.leonardoenglish.com.

00:23:17.473 --> 00:23:19.453
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:19.453 --> 00:23:22.163
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:22.623 --> 00:23:25.523
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:25.523 --> 00:23:28.023
I'll catch you in the next episode.

